\f1\fs22 Three hours later, Agent Jones was the good kind of angry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Earlier, the briefing with the new police Commissioner progressed much as expected, including the expected exchange of opening pleasantries.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What is you name, Agent?\'92 Gerry asked him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ajent Jones,\'92 was Agent Jones\'92 perfunctory reply.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry missed the significance of Ajent pronouncing his first name with a faux-sounding French accent, and so needed clarification. \'91No, I mean your given name. The name your parents gave you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ajent,\'92 Agent Jones repeated, refraining from sounding testy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Agent?\'92 Gerry asked, committing the parochial sin of making no attempt to copy the pronunciation. \'91Your name is Agent Jones?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91
\f2\i Arzhant,
\f1\i0 \'92 Agent Jones emphasised. \'91With a 
\f2\i zhay
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Very well, Special Agent Ajent Jones. Let\'92s go ahead with the briefing shall we?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The briefing went well enough and, if anything, Gerry demonstrated an above-average ability, for a police person, to retain and process information. 
\f2\i Suspiciously above average
\f1\i0 , Agent Jones thought. Gerry was sharp enough to assess Tony Supaporn as incapable of all the charges levelled at him and even wondered why he was still playing the starring role of an apparently never ending series of 
\f2\i Curb your Individualism 
\f1\i0 episodes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Need to know,\'92 Agent Jones said, expecting Gerry would object. Gerry merely set his jaw and nodded, and left it at that. 
\f2\i Suspicious
\f1\i0 . Agent Jones avoided any mention of the missing Gyges Disc, and Gerry seemed to be either unconcerned by it, or had no knowledge of it. Or was withholding. 
\f2\i Very suspicious
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time the briefing concluded and Agent Jones was ensconced back in his office, he was the good kind of angry. Not the kind of angry that he salved with the ritual phrases he often uttered before taking a life: 
\f2\i I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the Company\'92s will. In the name of stability I destabilize. In the name of order I deliver chaos. In the name of life I bring death.
\f1\i0  This was the kind of angry he could manage with a good doodle.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Agent Jones sat his desk and took out a large art pad and a fresh box of HB pencils from a deep drawer. He lay the pad flat and stroked the palms of both hands across it a couple of times, then emptied the pencils from the box onto the desk. He grabbed a 2B like he was strangling a duck and commenced to draw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Doodling helped him get his voice out, helped him to ideate, and helped him express. As he added spider webs to the corners the paper, ornate stars down the left margin and badly drawn kittens along the bottom fucking and stabbing each other with sharpened bed springs, he entered a meditative state in which his perpetual varieties of anger seem to dislodge and dissolve like jagged shards of toffee in warm water. His muscles relaxed, his bones formed a new frame and, after a few minutes, he ceased to exist.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When mentation returned to Agent Jones, he saw he had scribbled in the middle of the page:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry Hardman is a spy
\f1\i0 .\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

